INT. NIGHT-MARKET FISH STALL WALK-IN FREEZER - NIGHT

A steel freezer behind a night-market fish stall. Frost on every surface. Hanging carcasses of tuna sway slightly in the cold, their eyes sealed with ice. A single light panel hums. The air tastes of salt and metal.

VIVIAN CHOW, fifties, the stall owner, in a white rubber apron and gloves, stands with her back to a rack of frozen squid. Her face betrays nothing.

MARCUS DELACROIX-OYELARAN, forties, a loan collector in a long wool coat, stands directly in front of her, hands in his pockets. His breath smokes.

TWO-TONE EDDIE, thirties, his driver, a heavy man with a bleached streak in his hair, leans against the door, arms folded.

HANNAH PRZYBYLSKI, twenties, Vivian's niece, holds a tray of ice with trembling fingers, trying to look busy.

OLD MR. ZHAO, seventies, sits on an upturned crate in the corner with a cigarette he has not lit, watching.

MARCUS
Forty thousand, Mrs. Chow.

VIVIAN
I know the number.

MARCUS
The number has been the number for six months.

VIVIAN
The number is not on my table.

Eddie shifts his weight against the door. The metal creaks. Hannah's tray rattles. Zhao rolls the cigarette between his fingers.

MARCUS
Then where is it?

VIVIAN
In the fish.

MARCUS
(a thin smile)
In the fish.

VIVIAN
Every night, a little. In the bellies. You pay a man to carry it off the pier and nobody looks inside a tuna.

MARCUS
Clever.

VIVIAN
Thirty-one years in this trade.

He steps closer. Hannah backs up a pace and the tray tilts. A few cubes slide off and shatter on the floor. Nobody turns.

MARCUS
Show me.

Vivian picks up a long steel knife from the rack. Eddie straightens. Zhao lights his cigarette at last, the flame small and orange in all that white.

VIVIAN
Which one would you like?

MARCUS
Surprise me.

She walks along the line of tuna, touches one with the flat of her blade, then another. She stops at the last. She makes one long cut. Something falls out onto the ice, wrapped in oilcloth.

HANNAH
(a whisper)
Auntie.

VIVIAN
Hold the tray steady, child.

Marcus crouches, unwraps the oilcloth with two fingers. Inside is a thick bundle of cash and a small black notebook. He flips the notebook open. He reads a line. He reads another. His jaw tightens.

MARCUS
This is not my money.

VIVIAN
No.

MARCUS
This is a list.

VIVIAN
It is a list of every man your boss pays to look the other way. The names. The dates. Every Tuesday.

Eddie takes his hand off the door.

EDDIE
Marcus.

MARCUS
Not now.

EDDIE
Marcus, that's my cousin on page two.

MARCUS
I said not now.

ZHAO
(rough, quiet)
The market is closing. Everybody leave.

Nobody moves. The tuna sway. The light hums. Vivian wipes her blade clean on her apron, once, twice, and slides it back into the rack.

VIVIAN
You may keep the money. The book stays with me.

MARCUS
And if I refuse?

VIVIAN
Then you will tell your boss you walked into a freezer with a list of his friends and walked out without it.

Marcus closes the notebook. He holds it a long moment. He lays it back on the ice, carefully, as if it might wake.

MARCUS
Eddie, the door.

Eddie opens it. Warm night air rolls in, loud with frying oil and distant music. Marcus pockets the cash and goes out without looking back. Eddie follows.

The door swings shut. Hannah lets the tray fall.

HANNAH
Auntie, you were so calm.

VIVIAN
I was so cold.

Zhao exhales a long plume of smoke. Vivian picks up the notebook and tucks it in her apron pocket.
